gascoigne is a token optimizer for LLM agents. sits between an agent and what agent
reads (shell output, MCP tool results, web pages), shrinks it, and keeps every original in
local ledger nothing ever lost.

Output gascoigne already optimized (exec, its MCP server, the proxy) is recognized by its
hint and left alone, as gascoigne's own MCP tools. **Read** is never replaced: Claude
numbers lines shows, and dropped lines shift numbers. **WebSearch** is
left too: results (a median of 608 tokens in measured sessions) rendered by Claude
Code from fixed structure.

Measured on 16 Claude Code sessions (3,148 tool results): tool result stays in context for
**median 110 later requests** (mean 145, deciles 17–335), and 0.8% of gaps between
requests exceed 5-minute cache TTL. rereads, not first
cost: Opus 5.5 token saved worth $5/MTok at first read and $31/MTok
context.

Most new support is a **dialect**: a new tool in a family gascoigne already has. A new
**optimizer** is only for a new family. Other component kinds (receivers, filters,
exporters, extensions) follow the same package shape.

Every run appears as it happens, with its savings, content type and, when it was not
optimized, the reason. Click a run to compare the original with what the agent read, side by
side, and see each optimizer's step.

Ids are first 8 hex digits of text's sha256 (12, 16 or 64
text), same output from any session stored once and hint costs ~7
fewer tokens. Originals gzip-compressed. Source keys longer than 200 bytes (whole
heredocs) stored as 160-byte prefix plus hash. Ledgers older
versions converted on first open and vacuumed (a real one: 22 MB → 6 MB).

One self-contained page served from the binary, with no external requests. It follows the
system's light or dark theme; the *Auto / Light / Dark* switch in the header overrides it
and is remembered per browser.

run lost over 99.5% of large output gets warning to check it. optimized text
kept next to original (gzip, same retention), runs recorded before show only
original. not mark failing.
